% c262 (A&S Unit 27, PDF 469-470 sec. 7, Ex. 27-10: in an inverted V9 the 9th sounds with its tone of resolution; only wide spacing keeps it clear): 22 beat 2 Cl 1 A flat4 with the horns (was G4, a semitone under their A flat4); the root G stays in the violas and Bn 1 at G3.
\version "2.24.0"
% c342 (harmony; A&S Unit 31, PDF 561 sec. 8, Ex. 31-14 and 31-15 (images): an applied °7 on #4 'is itself often preceded and intensified by another °7 on natural 4', the first in 4/3 position, 'by far the most frequent and characteristic usage of consecutive rising °7s'; a °4/3 may stand for IV or II; PDF 537: an altered tone inside a chromatic line is less disruptive):
%   20 violas D4-B flat3-B3-C4 (was D4-F4-C4): 20.3 is vii°4/3 over F (F-A flat-B, Cl 1 A flat4, violas B3) instead of a bare iv, so F-F sharp, A flat-A and B-C rise by semitones into c322's VII°7 of V on 20.4; the B natural comes inside the violas' B flat-B-C line, not against Bn 1's B flat3. Tune, bass, recapitulation's 160 unchanged.
% c322 (A&S Unit 25, PDF 428 sec. 6 and Ex. 25-12a: #4 in the bass as a chromatic passing tone from IV to V, 'perhaps the most important function of chords applied to V'; PDF 425 Ex. 25-14: VII°7 of V to V with flat-3 on top needs a skip or a delayed voice to avoid d5-P5): 20 beats 3-4 iv (F) -> VII°7 of V (F sharp-A-C-E flat) -> V7 at 21; bass B flat-F-F sharp-G (Vc, Cb, Bn 2, Cbsn); Cl 1 A flat4-A4 (chromatic step in one voice); violas F4-C4 (C held as common tone); Hn 4 A3 skipping to F3, Bn 1 A3 to G3 on 21.2; Hn 3 keeps C4. The tune's E flat stays a seventh falling to D (c257); F1's notes unchanged; 160 (recapitulation, protected) unchanged.
% c259 (Adler part 1 ch. 5 PDF 152 and ch. 8 PDF 257, Ex. V-27, VIII-25: a first-inversion chord keeps its open sound with the root present and the doubled bass low): 20.1 Cl 1 G4 (was D4), the root of c257's v6 over B flat, held from 19's last eighth; the downbeat was the bare third B flat-D under the tune's B flat in five octaves.
% c258 (expression; Sebesky ch. 1 Ex. 2A, PDF 17-18: each layer of an orchestration carries its own word for the sound wanted, "(EASY)" on the harp's figure; PDF 13-14 focus): the broken-chord eighth accompaniment of F1 is marked "leggiero" at each entry (11 Cl 1-2, 19 violas, 83 violas, 151 Cl 1-2), so it stays under the tune that shares its register. Dynamics unchanged.
% c282 (A&S Unit 10, PDF 153-154, 158-160: a cadential 6/4 intensifies the antecedent's semicadence; its 4th enters as a common tone and falls by step to the 3rd on the next beat; the 6th falls to the 5th):
%   18 beat 1 Cl 2 C4 (held from 17's last eighth) and Cl 1 E flat4 (from F4) make the 6/4 G-C-E flat under the tune's G; both resolve to B3 and D4 on beat 2 with Hn 3 B3. Tune and bass unchanged.
% c332 (harmony; A&S Unit 29, PDF 528-530 sec. 19, Ex. 29-31a and 29-31c: #IV7, the diminished-third chord, has the scale degrees of the German 6th with #4 in the bass; it 'results from the use of #4 as a chromatic passing tone ... in the bass', 'can replace a diatonic subdominant before a cadential V', and its d3 F sharp-A flat contracts to the octave G; PDF 505: like the German 6th it shares two common tones with the cadential 6/4):
%   17 beats 3-4 iv -> #IV7 (F sharp-A flat-C-E flat) -> 18 cadential 6/4 -> V; bass F-F sharp-G (Vc, Cb, Bn 2) instead of the root-position ii°7 on D (c112's open point 16.3/17.3); Cl 1 C4-E flat4-A flat4-E flat4, Cl 2 E flat4 for D4, Hn 4 E flat3 for F3. The tune's A flat5 (17.3) is the chord's flat-6, falling to G against the bass's F sharp-G. F1, 157 (recapitulation) and c282's 6/4 unchanged.
% FIRST THEME (theme F1), bars 11-26, C minor, f. Strings with brass on the tune (cyclic-notes: the first theme does not start
% in the clarinet or a solo violin, and does not open with a rising sixth): 11-18 violins in unison, violas and horns 1-2 an octave lower;
% 19-26 Vln I an octave higher (with Fl 1), Vln II (with Ob 1) at the first octave, violas and horns 1-2 below.
% Antecedent 11-18 ends on V (half cadence); consequent 19-26 ends with a deceptive cadence V-VI at 26: no root-position V-i in C
% before the last cadence of the symphony (cyclic-notes: movement I has none after its bar 135).
% Clarinets: broken chords in eighths; horns 3-4 and bassoon 1: off-beat chords; cellos, basses and bassoon 2: the bass line.

% c058: 19 contrabassoon entry mf (as Bn 2 on the same bass line); it entered with no dynamic after the mf< of 9-10.
pOneVlnOne = { c''2->(\f g'4. aes'8 | bes'4-.) aes'8( g'8 f'4-.) es'4-. | d'4( g'8 aes'8 b'4 d''4) | f''2-> es''4( d''4) | es''2->( bes'4. c''8 | des''4-.) c''8( bes'8 aes'4-.) g'4-. | f'8( aes'8 c''8 f''8 aes''2) | g''2.-> r4 | c'''2->( g''4. aes''8 | bes''4-.) aes''8( g''8 f''4-.) es''4-. | d''4( f''8 aes''8 b''4 d'''4) | aes'''2-> g'''4( f'''4) | es'''2->( d'''4. c'''8 | b''4-.) c'''8( d'''8 g''4-.) f''4-. | es''4( d''8 c''8~ c''4 b'4) | c''2-> r2 | }
pOneVlnTwo = { c''2->(\f g'4. aes'8 | bes'4-.) aes'8( g'8 f'4-.) es'4-. | d'4( g'8 aes'8 b'4 d''4) | f''2-> es''4( d''4) | es''2->( bes'4. c''8 | des''4-.) c''8( bes'8 aes'4-.) g'4-. | f'8( aes'8 c''8 f''8 aes''2) | g''2.-> r4 | c''2->( g'4. aes'8 | bes'4-.) aes'8( g'8 f'4-.) es'4-. | d'4( f'8 aes'8 b'4 d''4) | aes''2-> g''4( f''4) | es''2->( d''4. c''8 | b'4-.) c''8( d''8 g'4-.) f'4-. | es'4( d'8 c'8~ c'4 b4) | c'2-> r2 | }
% c181 (A&S Unit 20, PDF 340 section 15; PDF 341 section 16; A&S Unit 13 PDF 202): 25-26 the violas' figure no longer doubles the tune's suspended C and leading tone B and no longer falls B3-A flat3
% (an augmented second, the leading tone dropping to the sixth at the deceptive cadence); 25 G-D-F-D, 26 C-E flat-A flat-E flat (D4 to C4 by step, no unison or octave with Vln II, Cl 1 or the bass).
pOneVla = { c'2->(\f g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( g8 aes8 b4 d'4) | f'2-> es'4( d'4) | es'2->( bes4. c'8 | des'4-.) c'8( bes8 aes4-.) g4-. | f8( aes8 c'8 f'8 aes'2) | g'2.-> r4 | g8(\mf^\markup \italic "leggiero" c' es' c') g8( b c' es') | d'4( bes4 b4 c'4) | g8( b d' b) g8( b d' b) | g8( b d' b) g8( b d' b) | g8( c' es' c') aes8( d' f' d') | d'1 | b8( d' f' d') g8( d' f' d') | c'8( es' aes' es') r2 | }
% c095 (Gould PDF 21: stay in one clef using up to at least three ledger lines; PDF 23): the bass clef at 25-26 is score-only. At concert pitch E flat3-B2 needs
% 3-5 ledger lines in treble, but in the F parts the same bars read B flat3-F sharp3 (at most three), so the parts stay in treble with no clef change. 25 (and 165): E flat3 stem up, as the D-C eighths in the same half-bar (Gould PDF 28).
pOneHnOne = { c'2->(\mf g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( g8 aes8 b4 d'4) | f'2-> es'4( d'4) | es'2->( bes4. c'8 | des'4-.) c'8( bes8 aes4-.) g4-. | f8( aes8 c'8 f'8 aes'2) | g'2.-> r4 | c'2->(\f g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( f8 aes8 b4 d'4) | aes'2-> g'4( f'4) | es'2->( d'4. c'8 | b4-.) c'8( d'8 g4-.) f4-. | \tag #'score \clef bass \once \stemUp es4( d8 c8~ c4 b,4) | c2-> r2 \tag #'score \clef treble | }
pOneHnTwo = { c'2->(\mf g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( g8 aes8 b4 d'4) | f'2-> es'4( d'4) | es'2->( bes4. c'8 | des'4-.) c'8( bes8 aes4-.) g4-. | f8( aes8 c'8 f'8 aes'2) | g'2.-> r4 | c'2->(\f g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( f8 aes8 b4 d'4) | aes'2-> g'4( f'4) | es'2->( d'4. c'8 | b4-.) c'8( d'8 g4-.) f4-. | \tag #'score \clef bass \once \stemUp es4( d8 c8~ c4 b,4) | c2-> r2 \tag #'score \clef treble | }
% c394 (orchestration; Adler part 2 ch. 15, PDF 151, Mozart 40/iv Ex. XV-3: put each instrument where every note it plays is effective, not left at the unison where it adds nothing to heavy string scoring;
%   PDF 151-152, Beethoven 9/i Ex. XV-4: the flutes alone carry a superimposed top octave, which needs no other doubling; PDF 150, Barber note 2): 24.3-26 Fl 1 keeps its octave above Vln I
%   (G6-F6 | E flat6-D6-C6-B5 | C6) instead of following the tune down to E flat5-B4 in unison with Vln I and both oboes under the trombones' ff entry at 25; the C6 at 26 leads into the countersubject's C6 at 27.
pOneFluteOne = { R1*8 | c'''2->(\f g''4. aes''8 | bes''4-.) aes''8( g''8 f''4-.) es''4-. | d''4( f''8 aes''8 b''4 d'''4) | aes'''2-> g'''4( f'''4) | es'''2->( d'''4. c'''8 | b''4-.) c'''8( d'''8 g'''4-.) f'''4-. | es'''4( d'''8 c'''8~ c'''4 b''4) | c'''2-> r2 | }
% c041 (Adler ch. 7, PDF 198: the oboe's lowest fifth B flat3-F4 is "thick, heavy" and honks): 24 (beat 3)-26 Ob 1 joins Fl 1 and Vln I an octave higher (G5 ... C5) instead of ending the consequent on B3-C4; same for both oboes at 164-166 (recP.ily).
pOneOboeOne = { R1*8 | c''2->(\f g'4. aes'8 | bes'4-.) aes'8( g'8 f'4-.) es'4-. | d'4( f'8 aes'8 b'4 d''4) | aes''2-> g''4( f''4) | es''2->( d''4. c''8 | b'4-.) c''8( d''8 g''4-.) f''4-. | es''4( d''8 c''8~ c''4 b'4) | c''2-> r2 | }
% c187 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 75: when two horns play the lower octave in unison, three or four wind instruments are needed above, especially in forte):
% 19-26 Ob 2 joins Ob 1 a2 on F1 at Vln II's octave, so the f horns' C4 octave (bare since the violas took the figure, c057) has Fl 1 + Ob 1-2 above it.
pOneOboeTwo = { R1*8 | c''2->(\f g'4. aes'8 | bes'4-.) aes'8( g'8 f'4-.) es'4-. | d'4( f'8 aes'8 b'4 d''4) | aes''2-> g''4( f''4) | es''2->( d''4. c''8 | b'4-.) c''8( d''8 g''4-.) f''4-. | es''4( d''8 c''8~ c''4 b'4) | c''2-> r2 | }
% c105 (engraving; Gould, Behind Bars PDF 36 (c) and PDF 37: a group whose inner note lies nearer the beam than both outer notes is concave and takes a horizontal beam;
% PDF 38: in a chord the note nearest the beam decides; PDF 37: repeated figuration looks better horizontal): every clarinet group here (E flat-G-C-G, D-F-G-F, D-G-B-G, F-A flat-C-A flat ...) is concave,
% but LilyPond sloped them about half a space. Staff-level damping makes them horizontal on the shared staff and in each part; reverted at the end of the passage.
% c361 (A&S Unit 20, PDF 330-331 sec. 4 and Ex. 20-5: 'In most situations, 5ths and octaves created by chordal skips count as faulty voice leading and must be avoided. Never write chordal skips without checking for forbidden parallels'; PDF 341 sec. 16: 5ths produced by a consonant tone, like those of a chordal skip, are to be avoided):
%   19.3 Cl 1 B3 (was D4): its chordal skip G4 (19.2.5) -> D4 on beat 3 made fifths with F1's head in Hn 1-2 (C4 -> G3), on the head's main notes; B3 is the passing V6/4's third (c313), taken over by the violas on 19.3.5. Figure and slur unchanged otherwise.
pOneClOne = { \override Staff.Beam.damping = #+inf.0 es'8(\mf^\markup \italic "leggiero" g' c'' g') es'8( g' c'' g') | f'2( aes'2) | d'8( f' g' f') d'8( f' g' f') | d'8( f' g' f') d'8( g' b' g') | es'8( g' c'' g') es'8( g' bes' g') | aes'2( f'2) | f'8( aes' c'' aes') c'8( es' aes' es') | es'8( g' d' g') r2 | es'8( g' c'' g') b8( g' es' g') | g'2( aes'4 a'4) | d'8( f' g' f') d'8( f' g' f') | d'8( f' aes' f') d'8( g' b' g') | es'8( g' c'' g') d'8( f' aes' f') | g'2( f'2) | d'8( f' g' d') d'8( f' g' f') | es'8( aes' c'' aes') r2 | \revert Staff.Beam.damping }
% c443 (A&S Unit 15, PDF 236 sec. 6 and Ex. 15-9: the root-position diminished II is avoided; PDF 239 sec. 12, Ex. 15-17: a diminished 5/3 is seldom satisfactory, the missing tone is supplied): 16.3 was a bare D-F-A flat (Hn 3's C only on the off-beat, Cl 1 doubling the tune's A flat 4). Cl 1 now falls A flat4-F4 and Cl 2 F4-C4, so the half bar is a complete ii7 (half-dim.) on the beat, as 156.3 already is (Tbn 1 D flat-C); Cl 1 reaches 17's F4 by common tone.
pOneClTwo = { \override Staff.Beam.damping = #+inf.0 g8(\mf-\tag #'part ^\markup \italic "leggiero" c' es' c') g8( c' es' c') | d'2( c'2) | g8( b d' b) g8( b d' b) | g8( b d' b) g8( b d' b) | g8( c' es' c') g8( bes es' bes) | f'2( c'2) | aes8( c' f' c') aes8( c' es' c') | c'8( es' b d') r2 | R1*8 | \revert Staff.Beam.damping }
% c447 (texture; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. III, PDF 108: in soft four-part harmony of horns and bassoons, cross the parts, 'the concords being given to the horns, the discords to the bassoons', bassoons below or inside the horns; 'in a chain of consecutive chords ... the stationary parts to the brass, the moving parts to the wood-wind'):
%   13-15 and 21-23 the mf off-beat chords over V7 had the seventh F3 in Hn 4 and Bn 1 doubling the root G3. Hn 4 now holds G3 under Hn 3's D4 (13-14, 21-22) and Bn 1 takes F3 below them,
%   resolving it to E flat3 on 15.2 and 23.2, so the off-beat C-minor chord there has its third (was C4-G3-G3). 24-26 unchanged (Hn 4's F3-E flat3 into the deceptive VI). Tune, bass, recapitulation unchanged.
pOneHnThree = { r4 c'4-.\mf r4 c'4-. | r4 d'4-. r4 c'4-. | r4 d'4-. r4 d'4-. | r4 d'4-. r4 d'4-. | r4 c'4-. r4 es'4-. | r4 des'4-. r4 c'4-. | r4 c'4-. r4 c'4-. | r4 b4-. r2 | r4 c'4-. r4 c'4-. | r4 d'4-. r4 c'4-. | r4 d'4-. r4 d'4-. | r4 d'4-. r4 d'4-. | r4 c'4-. r4 d'4-. | r4 d'4-. r4 d'4-. | r4 d'4-. r4 d'4-. | r4 c'4-. r2 | }
pOneHnFour = { r4 g4-.\mf r4 g4-. | r4 f4-. r4 f4-. | r4 g4-. r4 g4-. | r4 g4-. r4 g4-. | r4 g4-. r4 g4-. | r4 f4-. r4 f4-. | r4 f4-. r4 es4-. | r4 g4-. r2 | r4 g4-. r4 g4-. | r4 g4-. r4 a4-. | r4 g4-. r4 g4-. | r4 g4-. r4 g4-. | r4 g4-. r4 f4-. | r4 g4-. r4 f4-. | r4 f4-. r4 f4-. | r4 es4-. r2 | }
pOneBsnOne = { \partCombineApart r4 g4-.-\tweak stencil ##f \mf r4 g4-. | r4 bes4-. r4 aes4-. | r4 f4-. r4 f4-. | r4 f4-. r4 f4-. | r4 es4-. r4 bes4-. | r4 aes4-. r4 aes4-. | r4 aes4-. r4 aes4-. | r4 g4-. r2 | r4 g4-. r4 g4-. | r4 bes4-. r4 a4-. | r4 f4-. r4 f4-. | r4 f4-. r4 f4-. | r4 es4-. r4 aes4-. | r4 g4-. r4 g4-. | r4 g4-. r4 g4-. | r4 c'4-. r2 \partCombineAutomatic | }
